    ],
  },
  {
    title: '17. Flow Subscriptions and Add-ons',
    paragraphs: [
      'Flow Base keeps Markdown reading, editing and saving free. Flow Pro is a paid subscription, billed monthly or yearly through Stripe. Flow Import and Flow Publish are optional paid add-ons to Pro.',
      FLOW_CANCEL_TERMS,
      FLOW_ADDON_TERMS,
      FLOW_TRIAL_TERMS,
      'If Pro ends, your documents stay yours. They stay readable and editable, and past receipts stay readable.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: '18. Product Mocks and Product Shots',
    paragraphs: [
      'Our pages show Flow in two ways. A product mock is a drawing of Flow’s screens, built into the web page. Some mocks move or respond when you click them. A product shot is a real screenshot of the Flow app.',
      'Product mocks appear on the homepage, the Flow page, the Manifesto, the Paths and Compare pages, and in essays. They use fictional sample data. A mock shows how Flow works, but it is not the app. It may make a screen simpler, skip steps, or look different from the version of Flow you have.',
      'Product shots appear in the Flow tour, on Flow feature pages such as Night Shift and Living Documents, and in path walkthroughs. They are real screenshots of Flow. Where we can, the caption or the page names the Flow version they came from. The documents in shots also use fictional sample data.',
      'Why we use mocks: Flow changes fast. From August 29 to September 28, 2026, we shipped 15 Flow releases, from 1.5.6 to 2.0.3. Four of them were feature releases: 1.6, 1.7, 1.8 and 2.0. At that pace a screenshot can be out of date within days. A mock lets us show how Flow works without passing off an old screen as the current one.',
      'Here is what you can rely on. The Flow app, its release notes, and the terms for your plan describe what Flow does. A mock does not. If a mock and the app differ, the app and its release notes control. Numbers we measured, such as how long a task took or what it cost on a Mac, come from real runs of Flow, and each one links to the walkthrough it came from.',
      'If you think a mock or a shot shows more than Flow does, tell us at manav@orionfold.com and we will fix the page.',
    ],
  },
];
---
<PaperLayout
  title="Terms of Use · Orionfold products and site"
  description="Terms of Use for the Orionfold website, software, downloads, and related services."
  ogImage={og.image}
  ogImageAlt={og.alt}
  jsonLd={[
    {
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'TermsOfService',
      name: 'Terms of Use · Orionfold',
      url: `${SITE.url}/terms/`,
      inLanguage: 'en',
      publisher: PUBLISHER,
      dateModified: '2026-09-29',
    },
    {
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Terms', item: `${SITE.url}/terms/` },
      ],
    },
  ]}
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" class="pp-page">
    <PaperLongform crumb={{ href: '/trust/', label: 'Trust' }} kicker="Legal" title="Terms of Use" dek="Practical rules for using Orionfold's open AI software, models, and services. This version is written to reduce ambiguity, set clear responsibility boundaries, and avoid overpromising." author={{ name: 'Orionfold LLC', href: '/about/' }} updated={updated} headings={sections.map((s) => ({ depth: 2, slug: slugify(s.title), text: s.title }))} magnet={false}>
      <aside class="pp-callout" aria-label="The short version">
        <p class="pp-kicker">The short version</p>
        <p>Orionfold is provided as open-source software, models, and related services, but you remain responsible for what you run, what you connect, and what you decide to rely on.</p>
      </aside>
      <ol class="pp-numbered">
        {summaryCards.map((card) => <li><strong>{card.title}.</strong> {card.body}</li>)}
      </ol>
      {sections.map((section) => (
        <Fragment>
          <h2 id={slugify(section.title)}>{section.title}</h2>
          {section.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          {section.bullets && <ul>{section.bullets.map((bullet) => <li>{bullet}</li>)}</ul>}
        </Fragment>
      ))}
      <div slot="rail" class="pp-rail-block">
        <p class="pp-kicker">Questions</p>
        <p class="pp-rail-text">Email <a href="mailto:manav@orionfold.com">manav@orionfold.com</a>. A person reads every message.</p>
      </div>
    </PaperLongform>
  </main>
  <Footer showDownload={false} />
</PaperLayout>
